<<~ ahu #/the-measurement-first>>

! The measurement, before the design (a dated record, 2026-09-13; `OpenIdentitySlot` leaves the tree at #/the-ruling)

The standing reading calls `OpenIdentitySlot.verifyCapability`'s `return true; // alpha: open` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/identity-slot.ts:134-136`) the most-urgent crypto hole. ''Measured, it reaches no live path at all.''

* The ONE call site of any slot's `verifyCapability` in source stands at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/federation-gate.ts:192`, inside `identityShareDecision`.
* That line runs only past `:186` — `if (!identity) return true;`.
* The BROWSER holds no identity-ring socket: `browserShareConfig` reads the federation gate alone, through `federationShareDecision`, and the PersonaGroup ring composes onto that gate (`open-browser-vessel.ts`, the `assemblePersonaGroupRing` call).
* The NODE hands its whole verdict to `selfSlotShareDecision`, which passes `null` for the identity ring at BOTH exits (`self-slot-share.ts:91-100` · `:103`) — and says why in its own header: the split rides the OUTER deterministic gate, never the inner verify-for-self ring, ''sidestepping the allow-all self-grant trap''.
* `OpenIdentitySlot` is constructed in exactly one place, `lar-vessel.ts:98`, and nothing reads `vessel.identity` to build an `IdentityRing`.

''So the socket stands unwired, and the stub's allow-all is dead code on every live path.'' That matters for how the cure is ranked: nothing leaks today through this line, and lighting the ring NAIVELY would introduce the regression `federation-gate.ts:238-241` already warns of — the self-slot answers //may I sync my own doc// with the presenter equal to self, which reads as an allow-all the moment it is consulted.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-decides-today>>

!! What decided a verdict at the measurement, in order

The slot is absent from this stack entirely.

# ''the deterministic set'' (`federation-gate.ts:78-96`) — a pure function of the Nexus gate pubkey: crossroads · WHO · Kapae-antigen · carriage · persona-KEL boards, plus caller-supplied extras. Set membership, nothing else.
# ''the peer ring'' (`:108-118`) — an in-process island peer syncs whole; a same-operator relay peer syncs whole; a doc-less ask denies.
# ''the antigen ring'' (`:205-224`) — the quorum-signed Kapae denylist against the presenter's resolved nym. Additive deny only.
# ''the members board'' (`nexus-carriage.ts:219-225`) — the folded carriage contracts, keyed on the PROVEN contract nym ahead of the raw wire key. Fail-closed: unknown reads STRANGER. This ring folds an allow set, and the presented-admit ruling retires it as the gate — the subject presents its quorum-signed admit against the deny-only antigen ring — with the build owed (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>).
# ''the plane seal'' (`:295-298`) — provable ciphertext, fail-closed.
# ''the realm leg'' (`realm-bag.ts:490-512`) — the charter's own hearth · the registration's `keptBy` stewards · `holdsCharter(nym, charterId)` at CONTRACT · a faceless carrier PLACE at `readTier === "public"` alone · else the members board.
# ''the posture'' (`:405-424`) — `private` by default; `open` admits cross-operator.

''And the tier ladder that would carry an answer is itself unlit.'' `capTierShareDecision` (`:485-506`) has no live caller — grep finds it in `cap-tier.test.ts` alone — and `declaredTierForDoc` returns null for every doc because no bag carries a `capTier` datum. `structuralFloorFor` (`cap-tier.ts:187-193`) therefore governs, floored at VEIL.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-slot-could-key-on>>

!! What the slot could key on, with the realm leg standing

Three readings are available to a share verdict, and NONE of them is a roster (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter` — a compiled-about registry at any scope rejects the fork).

# ''THE CAP TIER, resolved.'' `resolveTierForDoc(declared, floor) = meet(declared, floor)` (`cap-tier.ts:246-250`) — declared may only TIGHTEN, and `mayDeclassify` (`:135-138`) makes loosening a one-way ratchet requiring operator-root. `tierPermitsRelayPeer` (`:267-273`) then answers: `public` → any relay peer · `contract` and `personagroup` → `holdsCarriagePeer` only · `veil` → false. ''This reading stands built and needs only lighting'' — a declared datum on the bag and a live caller.
# ''THE REALM REGISTRATION.'' A doc the realm names carries its own declared tier and its own `keptBy`; `RealmBagGate.mayFederate` already reads both. An identity-slot-shaped doc could register at CONTRACT and inherit the whole leg — steward-write, charter-read, carrier-never.
# ''THE PERSONA PLANE'S OWN MEMBERSHIP.'' The reading the task asks for — //a member of that persona's plane is admitted, a stranger refused//. ''It does not exist in code.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-fork-that-wants-a-ruling>>

!! ★ The fork — which plane owns the verdict ★

''The persona-plane reading has no reader, and the shape of the missing reader is itself the ruling.''

Measured: no function in `packages/lararium-mesh/src` answers //does this peer stand in this persona's plane//. `persona-planes.ts` and `persona-scope.ts` are name-derivation and LOCAL family logic — zero occurrences of `peerId` in any `persona-*.ts` file. `personaGroupMembers` and `membersOf` do not exist. A persona plane is protected today by sitting OUTSIDE the deterministic federatable set and by the same-operator peer class, never by a reader that knows which persona a peer belongs to.

''And the wire cannot tell the two apart.'' `cap-tier.ts:271` says it in its own comment: the shore ''cannot split fleet from cabal'', so `contract` and `personagroup` collapse to the same `holdsCarriagePeer` answer. A persona-plane verdict would need a distinction the transport does not carry.

So the fork stands, and it is a ruling and not a patch — the three answers differ in WHO the authority is:

* ''(A) THE VEIL OWNS IT.'' An identity-slot doc floors at VEIL and never leaves the vessel; the fleet reaches it by the same-operator class alone, which is a DEVICE answer and not a persona one. Cheapest, and it forecloses a genuinely shared account.
* ''(B) THE PERSONAGROUP OWNS IT.'' The slot keys on the persona plane's own membership — which needs the reader above, and needs the wire to carry a persona distinction it does not carry. Truest to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/face-planes` (relations travel with the FACE), and the most build.
* ''(C) THE REALM OWNS IT.'' The doc registers on the realm at CONTRACT and the realm leg answers; the persona never enters the verdict. Reuses everything that stands, and it puts a CABAL between a person and their own identity material — which `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane#/the-truncated-chain` already rules out for credentials by TYPE, and an identity slot sits adjacent to that refusal.

''The reach plane's own ceiling is the nearest standing law and it points at (A)-or-(B), never (C):'' a reach credential fleet-syncs and delivers to a contracted set, ''and it crosses no Nexus boundary in either case''; PUBLIC is refused by type, and the truncation falls out of a FLOOR (`isContractSecretsPlane` read FIRST, ahead of the public reading, `cap-tier.ts:189`) rather than a ceiling, so protection by omission never enters. Whether an identity SLOT reads as that same kind of thing is the operator's call, and it decides the fork.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-ruling>>

! ★ The fork CLOSED — arm (B), 2026-09-13 ★

The operator ruled: ''a slot doc's verdict = the face's grant records (the joinee's own act), never the realm's `keptBy`, never a roster; the realm never reads persona planes.''

!! What stands

`makePersonaGroupIdentityRing` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-ring.ts`) — the reader this carrier measured as missing. Pure, and it reads exactly three things, each injected: the docs it GOVERNS (this face's own planes), the vessel key a peer PROVED at this vessel's gate, and the grant records resting on the PersonaGroup plane with the seal check that judges one. That check rides in as a function, so `verifyFaceGrantRecord` — the same verify the joinee's own kit runs — answers here too, and mesh takes no keyhive dependency to do it.

''It refuses the other two readings by having no field for them.'' No realm input, so a name in a registration's `keptBy` grants nothing (its own CONTROL). No roster, so the registry filter never fires.

''It widens and never narrows.'' `compose(base)` is the realm leg's shape: the base's allow stands untouched and the ring adds ONE admit path. A wired ring can open a door and can never close one that stood — pinned against a permissive base.

''And the red never measures the base.'' This carrier's own warning (#/what-a-red-would-need) said the stranger half already passes for a reason that has nothing to do with the slot; every vector asks `admitsPeer` directly, so the ring alone decides.

No `OpenIdentitySlot` stands — no alias, the alpha line's own law. `LarVessel.identity` reads `IdentitySlot | null`, and a vessel given none carries none; no slot grants everything.

!! Two shores, one assembly

`assemblePersonaGroupRing` (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/persona-group-ring-assembly.ts`) hands the ring its inputs, and BOTH vessel shores call it — the node at `open-node-vessel.ts`, composed onto its self-slot fed gate; the browser at `open-browser-vessel.ts`, composed onto its cross-operator gate. It reads the face's four planes off the vessel's own catalog (`governedPlaneDocIds`), the face-join grant records on the PersonaGroup plane, and the founder's persona-KEL, and it binds the ONE `verifyFaceGrantRecord`. `tools/vessel-parity-witness.sh` holds the two shores to it.

''Only the PROOF SOURCE differs, and neither source makes a claim.''

* A NODE listens. Its `DaemonAuthGate` proves the dialer's key (the V3 proof) and the ring reads the identifier that gate proved.
* A LEAF dials out. The gate it dials signs its `lar:auth-ok` with the gate key over the leaf's own fresh nonce (`authOkBytes`, domain `auth-ok`), the leaf checks it against the key it pinned, and `LarWSClientAdapter.provenKeyOf` names that key for the peer the verified socket yields. A gate that cannot sign under the pin answers nothing the leaf reads as a pass. Authentication runs both ways on one socket.
* Two LEAVES of one PersonaGroup meet through a herm. Each proves its device key to the other (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/leaf-peer-proof.ts`): its DeviceDelegation edge, licensed by the verifier's own persona-KEL head and the lease epoch the verifier holds (an edge a rotated-away op-key signed reads as rolled past; an edge bound below the held epoch reads as lapsed), plus a signature by the device key over the exchange's whole transcript — both nonces, both ephemeral keys, the prover's role. The PersonaGroup secret gates every box (#/the-persona-group-secret): a hello carries a hint only a member computes, and every box mixes the secret into its key, so a herm reads no leaf↔root link even when it acts — a hello it forges draws no box. The proof binds the session it admits (`LeafPeerSession`): both siblings derive per-direction keys from the very ephemerals their device keys signed, every later frame seals under them, and a hash chain orders the frames, so a herm that injects, replays or reorders one meets a refusal that names the relay as its suspect, and both siblings close the session and prove again. `SiblingNetworkAdapter.provenKeyOf` names the device key a sibling proved.

''No clock enters any of the three.'' Freshness rides a nonce the verifying side chose; licensing rides KEL event order and the lease epoch the verifier holds — `standSiblingChannel` hands the proof the vessel's own read of it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-leaf-taxonomy>>

!! The leaf taxonomy — where the ring runs

The ring runs on EVERY vessel that holds a face, because a leaf comes in more than one kind. The operator's taxonomy names three:

# ''A face of an operator's PersonaGroup where a listening vessel exists.'' The leaf dials a node of its own PersonaGroup; that node's gate proves the leaf, and the node's signed verdict proves the node back.
# ''A single user device.'' One leaf, one face, dialing the hearth that hosts it. The same two-way proof on the one socket.
# ''One device in a user-caps-only fleet, where no vessel listens.'' No node stands to prove anyone, so the fleet's devices prove each other leaf to leaf through a relay that reads nothing (#/the-ruling, the third proof source). This kind explains why the ring must run on leaves at all: with no listening vessel, no other hand could decide. Each device dials a herm's relay through the knock its pinned gate key derives, proves its device key at that one gate, and joins its PersonaGroup's sibling channels — one per secret it holds, each tag an HMAC under that secret over the herm's gate key (`siblingChannelTag`), so a non-member holding the group's id computes none and two herms see two tags. Siblings run the proof through the herm, and Automerge sync rides only the session a passing proof binds (`SiblingNetworkAdapter`, in the automerge-repo `NetworkAdapter` shape), under the repo peer id the proven device key derives (`siblingPeerIdOf`): a sibling naming any other id is refused, and an id the repo already routes through another adapter gives it no route. Both vessel shores compose it by one call (`standSiblingChannel`), and the face's own KEL board re-licenses every standing session as it moves.

''A proven sibling holds STANDING, and nothing more.'' The PersonaGroup ring's sibling path (`composeSiblings`, reading the device key the sibling session proved) admits it to this face's own planes and the public boards — never the vessel's @daemon, and never another face's planes. A vessel that wears many faces keeps each face's siblings to that face; neither shore shares a sibling freely, and both read sibling-ness before any other ring, so an id a sibling and another carrier both claim denies. An impostor, a sibling whose lease lapsed, and a frame the herm forged each meet a refusal that surfaces and names its suspect (`peer`, `relay`, `route`, or `self` by cause: `revoked`, `unreadable`, `unsealed`); none drops in silence. A KEL head that rolls a sibling's edge past closes the session inside it and the pair proves again under the head. A leaf its own KEL revokes says goodbye inside every session it held, so no sibling stands half-open. A KEL that does not verify revokes no one: the leaf names it `unreadable` and keeps the standing a verified KEL gave it.

''The threshold keeps two faces'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/system-pattern-integrities#/threshold-pattern-integrities">>):

* ''Outward — the herm's face: carriage of sealed frames.'' The herm routes on the channel tags and the key its gate proved, and holds no secret, no session key, no edge in the clear — even when it acts, since a hello it forges draws no box — no group id and no document byte. Every act it takes on a session (a forged `here`, an injected frame, a frame reflected to its own sender) surfaces as a refusal that names the relay.
* ''Inward — the siblings' face: the PersonaGroup secret and the proven session.'' Only members hold the secret, and only the two siblings hold the session's keys. The herm's stamp of a sender key must agree with the proven edge, and it never stands in for one.

Nothing of the inward face rides the outward one.

A leaf holds only its own device key, its own edge, its sealed secret and its PersonaGroup's KEL; none of the three proofs asks it for a key it does not hold, and no persona root signs anything on any wire.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-persona-group-secret>>

!! The PersonaGroup secret — what keeps a forged hello from reading an edge

* ''Delivered at enrolment.'' When the persona root signs a DeviceDelegation it seals the group's secret to that device key beside the edge, in the same act (`enrolDevice`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-secret.ts`) — at founding, at device admit, and inside a contracted binding. The device's own daemon doc rests the seal, keyed by the group; it never crosses.
* ''Derived from the root's own seed, never from the KEL.'' The KEL rides public boards. The secret derives from the op-key's seed, salted by the KEL prefix, so the root reads it again whenever it signs and keeps no copy.
* ''Rolled with the op-key, sealed and attested on the KEL.'' A rotation seats a fresh op-key, whose seed derives the next secret, and in the same act re-enrols every remaining device: one box per device, sealed to that device's key, holding its re-delegated edge and the next secret, signed by the fresh op-key and naming no device (`rollEnrolments`). The KEL rides public boards, so no edge rides it in the clear; each device finds its own box by trial-open. The digest of the whole list rides inside the event's content address and the guardians' quorum bytes, so a board writer who strips, adds, swaps or reorders a box breaks the chain's walk and revokes no one. A device reads an edge out of its box only when that edge verifies under the event's op-key. A revoking rotation leaves the revoked device out: no box opens for it, it holds no secret under the new head, stands revoked and says so.
* ''A stale sibling catches up inside the seal.'' A sibling whose KEL has not yet seen a rotation says hello under its older secret, and the sibling ahead answers with the KEL suffix sealed under that secret. The stale side verifies the suffix onto its own head, lands it on its board, opens the secret the rotation sealed to it and proves again under the newest. A leaf keeps its older secrets and their channels, so the fleet never wedges on a rotation.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-a-red-would-need>>

!! What the smallest red would need, and why none is written here

The asked-for red — //a stranger asking an identity-slot doc is refused; a member of that persona's plane is admitted; the founder's own reads unchanged// — could not stand honestly while the fork stood open:

* the ''stranger'' half already passes, for a reason that has nothing to do with the slot: an identity-slot doc sits outside the deterministic federatable set and a WS peer absent from `peerIdentifierMap` draws `false` at `open-node-vessel.ts:497`. A red that goes green on that answer measures the fed gate and names the slot — the exact instrument-lie shape.
* the ''member of that persona's plane'' half has no function to call. Writing one binds the fork above, which is the ruling.
* the ''founder's own reads'' CONTROL stands as the one half worth keeping whichever way the fork closes: the same-operator class answer must not move.

''Once the fork closes, the red that pins it is ONE vector per arm'': under (A), a fleet device reads and a contracted cabal member does not; under (B), a member of the persona's plane reads and a fleet device of a DIFFERENT persona does not — the vector that proves the wire carries the distinction at all, and one that could not pass at the measurement; under (C), a charter holder reads and a carrier place does not. Each arm's CONTROL is the other two arms' admit drawing a refusal.

<<~/ahu>>

